1. For this lab, I implemented Insertion Sort and Merge Sort based on the mySort() function interface. Both algorithms were implemented using the 
provided metric methods to track the number of comparisons, moves, and swaps performed during sorting. After testing each algorithm, there are 
currently no known bugs on the files and compiles successfully. 

2. For both sorting methods, the number of swaps is 0 since insertion sort does not need any swaps. Merge sort uses temporary 
arrays and does not use swaps either. 

For insertion sort's number of comparisons worst case, the formula is n(n-1)/2. This is because the comparisons are in the while loop which will 
run n - 1 times. In the worst iteration, the while loop runs n times. This causes the series to be n(n-1)/2. 
For the best case, the formula is n - 1. This is because the while loop will iterate n - 1 times since it only needs to check and not to sort. 
For the average case, the formula is n(n-1)/4. This is because the while loop will run on an average of j/2 times since on average half of the 
elements in the array, array[j] will be > array[j + 1].

For insertion sort, the formula for number of copy operations in the worst case is (n-1)(n+4)/2. To get this, we can see that the 
first copy will pass n - 1 times. The while loop (inner) will run j times since this is the worst case. The total of this will be counted 
n - 1 (outer) times starting from j = 1. The series for that is n(n-1)/2. The last copy also runs n - 1 times meaning the total will be 2(n-1) + 
n(n-1)/2 which simplifies  to (n-1)(n+4)/2. 
In the best case, the formula will be 2(n-1). This is because the first and last copy will interate n - 1 times while the while loops copy will 
not run.
In the average case, the formula will be n(n-1)/4 + 2(n-1). This is because the while loop will run on an average of j/2 times since on average 
half of the elements in the array, array[j] will be > array[j + 1].

For merge sort, the formula will be nlog_2(n) - n + 1 for comparisons in the worst case. Each level of the recursion tree processes n elements 
total, and there are log2(n) levels, so that gives n log2(n) comparisons. However, since the comparisons are inside the while loop: 
(i <= mid && j <= last), we know that it will keep sorting and it will run until all units except one are sorted.
After that, we need to find out how many times this is called. Based on the recursion tree, the merge function will be called n - 1 times. 
So we need to subtract from the total, nlog_2(n), by how many times the while loop will run until one element is left which is n - 1 times.
For the best case, the formula is nlog_2(n)/2 since if one half of elements are all smaller than the other, that side will empty immediately 
without any further checks. This means it happens each time for the combination process which means each call will have a cost of n/2. For
the average case where numbers are randomly interleaved, on average the merge loop will run about n times which will lead to a formula
of nlog_2(n). Similarly to the worst case, the comparisons are inside the merge function which is called n - 1 times. This gives us a formula
closer to the worst case which is nlog_2(n) - n + 1. All cases are still nlog_2(n) as the parent function.

For merge sorts number of copies, the formula is 2nlog_2(n). For starters, the function has to copy everything into a temporary array n times, then 
copy the sorted temporary array back which is another n times. This happens accross nlog_2(n) levels which means that the formula is 2nlog_2(n) for 
all cases since merge sort always performs the same sequence of merge operations regardless of input order.

For both cases, if n is a number that gives decimals in log_2(n), then the result of that is rounded up to the nearest integer. 

